  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft remains relatively unchanged with a large \nand strong high pressure over the southeast CONUS and a developing \ntrough over northern California. Nearer to the surface, a small \npocket of low pressure resides over Wyoming. \n\nSpeaking of similarities, today will look very reminiscent of \nyesterday. An Extreme Heat Warning is in effect for the eastern 3 \ncounties from 1 to 9 PM with apparent temperatures exceeded 110 \ndegrees. A Heat Advisory is in effect for the same time for \nComanche, Clark, Edwards, Kiowa, and Pawnee counties with apparent \ntemperatures up to 105 degrees. This reflects the \nnorthwest/southeast gradient in temperatures. With areas near Scott \nCity, Hays, and Syracuse seeing highs around 95 degrees and Barber \ncounty exceeding 105 degrees. Bottom line is that it will continue \nto be hot. The storms chances tonight continue to eerily duplicate \nhow yesterday looked at this time. High-based storms with very \nlittle shear (10-20 KTs via forecast soundings) accompanying modest \nshear and moisture. The biggest difference in environment is that \nthe surface to 3 KM lapse rates are a little more favorable for \nstrong winds of 60-70 mph. However like yesterday, is only expected \nto be possible in the strongest storms if even at all. CAMs in the \nmost recent runs have the strongest storms north of the forecast \narea so the CWA may struggle to get severe winds again. Storms are \nforecast to begin to initiate around 5 PM near the Oklahoma/Kansas \nborder and move northeastward\n\nThis is pattern is forecast to remain remarkably consistent. \nTemperatures will be very similar on Saturday compared to Friday. \nThe storms are depicted by CAMs to also be very similar as the \nenvironment at-large remains nearly identical. The lack of shear is \nexpected to tamper any high end threats, but modest lapse rates and \nhigh CAPE could still generate severe hail and wind despite being \nhigher based storms. The NAMNST has some storms organizing and almost \nbowing as it moves northeastward towards Hays. This needs to be \nmonitored as it will likely host the most potent severe threat if it \nmaterializes (which should be taken skeptically between the uncertainty \nand variability. \n\nSunday will finally be much cooler relative to the past few days \nwith highs primarily in the 80s other than the warmer far \nsoutheastern counties. Yet again storm chances are possible with \nmost of the storms, and the ones with the most severe potential, \nbeing nearer to the Colorado/Kansas border. More time will need to \nelapse for CAMs to better resolve the progged storms and uncertainty \nto decease. \n\nMonday will be cooler, similar to Sunday, before Tuesday and the \nrest of the week warms back up. Ensembles seem to hint at a solid \nprecipitation signal during the Wednesday/Thursday overnight. \nMonitor for future updates regarding storms and the heat while \ntaking proper precautions!",
